# capability_iface.tkr — the §6 ACCEPT host: the interface-that-obliges-operator capability layer.
# Each scenario names itself and asserts its announced value through `run` in `main.tks`, so a relapse
# names itself. The program runs on BOTH backends (the native corpus + the own-vs-C differential),
# witnessing operators on a struct, the mandatory counter-part joint (`NeByEq`), and generic operator
# dispatch through an interface constraint (`<T: IEq>` / `<T: IOrd>`).

Feature: capability_iface — operators on struct/class/interface + generic dispatch by contract (§6/§9.4)

  Scenario: op_struct_eq — a struct's == and != dispatch to its own operator and the NeByEq counter-part (G1/G3)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario op_struct_eq: ok"

  Scenario: generic_index_of — index_of<T: IEq> dispatches == through the contract to Point::__eq (G4)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario generic_index_of: ok"

  Scenario: ne_by_eq — the negation counter-part flattened from NeByEq really dispatches (G5)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario ne_by_eq: ok"

  Scenario: ord_min2 — min2<T: IOrd> dispatches < and the GtByLt reflection answers > (G4)
    When built and run
    Then stdout pattern = "scenario ord_min2: ok"
